    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain.jpg" alt="Philip II of Spain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Philip_II_of_Spain_by_Sofonisba_Anguissola_-_002b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sofonisba Anguissola, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Dutch Republic grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, ruler of the seventeen provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Opposition to heavy taxation, centralisation and the persecution of Protestants hardened after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> arrived with a Spanish army in 1567 and set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-troubles">Council of Troubles</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, Prince of Orange and stadtholder of Holland, Zeeland and Utrecht, led armed resistance from 1568, and in 1572 the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brielle">Brielle</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-beggars">Sea Beggars</a> allowed the rebels to take most towns of Holland and Zeeland. The northern provinces bound themselves together in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a> in January 1579, a defensive alliance that served as the Republic’s constitution in all but name. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-abjuration">Act of Abjuration</a> of 26 July 1581 the States General renounced Philip II as their sovereign. The rebels offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> first to Francis, Duke of Anjou, and, after William the Silent was assassinated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a> in 1584, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I of England</a>, who declined it but sent troops under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-dudley-1st-earl-of-leicester">Earl of Leicester</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> left in 1587 the States General stopped seeking a monarch and governed as a republic of seven provinces, Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a>, Overijssel, Friesland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>, with conquered lands to the south ruled in common as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generality-lands">Generality Lands</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amsterdam.jpg" alt="Amsterdam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imagen_de_los_canales_conc%C3%A9ntricos_en_%C3%81msterdam.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrés Barrios, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic was the most urbanised society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: by the later seventeenth century more than half the people of Holland lived in towns. Its prosperity rested on the ‘mother trade’ with the Baltic, which brought grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> in exchange for herring, salt, wine and textiles, and on a large merchant fleet built cheaply in the Zaan shipyards around the fluyt, a cargo ship that needed only a small crew. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC), founded in 1602, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, founded in 1621, extended this commerce to Asia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> became the staple market of European trade and finance, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-amsterdam">Bank of Amsterdam</a> (1609) and an exchange where company shares were bought and sold. Agriculture was commercial and specialised, and land was won from lakes and the sea by windmill drainage, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beemster">Beemster</a> polder of 1612. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a> was the public church, but Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutherans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> could worship privately, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a> from Portugal settled in Amsterdam. Immigrants from the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, especially after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1585, brought capital and skills to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>’s textile industry and Amsterdam’s trade, and prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> sustained a large open market for paintings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nieuwpoort"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nieuwpoort.jpg" alt="Battle of Nieuwpoort" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nieuwpoort">Battle of Nieuwpoort</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prins_Maurits_in_de_Slag_bij_Nieuwpoort,_2_juli_1600,_SK-A-664.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paulus van Hillegaert (I), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Eighty Years’ War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> lasted, interrupted by the Twelve Years’ Truce of 1609–1621, until 1648. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a>, stadtholder from 1585, and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-louis-count-of-nassau-dillenburg">William Louis</a> reformed the army with regular pay, smaller units, constant drill and volley fire, and with the engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-stevin">Simon Stevin</a> they recaptured towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> in 1590 and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nieuwpoort">Battle of Nieuwpoort</a> in 1600. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-henry-prince-of-orange">Frederick Henry</a> continued the war of sieges, taking ’s-Hertogenbosch in 1629 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> in 1632, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fortification with earthen bastions and moats was copied across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. At sea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-heyn">Piet Heyn</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">West India Company</a> captured the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> fleet off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1628, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maarten-tromp">Maarten Tromp</a> destroyed a Spanish armada at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-downs">Battle of the Downs</a> in 1639. Commercial rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> produced three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-wars">Anglo-Dutch Wars</a> (1652–1654, 1665–1667 and 1672–1674); in 1667 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michiel-de-ruyter">Michiel de Ruyter</a> raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a> and towed away the English flagship <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-royal-charles-1655">Royal Charles</a></em>. In 1672 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, England, Münster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> attacked together, and the Republic survived by flooding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-water-line">Holland Water Line</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III</a> it then led coalitions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> in the Nine Years’ War and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, wars whose cost left Holland heavily indebted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johan-van-oldenbarnevelt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-van-oldenbarnevelt.jpg" alt="Johan van Oldenbarnevelt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johan-van-oldenbarnevelt">Johan van Oldenbarnevelt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johan_van_Oldenbarnevelt_(1547-1619),_by_workshop_of_Michiel_Jansz_van_Mierevelt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop of Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic was a confederation in which each of the seven provinces was sovereign. Provincial States, dominated in Holland by the regents of the voting towns alongside a delegation of nobles, sent deputies to the States General at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, which handled foreign policy, war and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generality-lands">Generality Lands</a>; each province had one vote, and major decisions required unanimity. Holland paid over half of the common budget, and its chief legal officer, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Land’s Advocate</a>, later called the Grand Pensionary, often directed policy, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-van-oldenbarnevelt">Johan van Oldenbarnevelt</a> did until his execution in 1619 after a clash with Maurice over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remonstrants">Remonstrant</a> controversy in the Reformed Church. The princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">Orange-Nassau</a> held the provincial office of stadtholder in most provinces and the command of the army, which gave them a quasi-monarchical standing. The rivalry between these two centres produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-stadtholderless-period">First Stadtholderless Period</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> died in 1650, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-de-witt">Johan de Witt</a> led the Republic as Grand Pensionary until the crisis of 1672, in which he and his brother Cornelis were lynched in The Hague. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-stadtholderless-period">Second Stadtholderless Period</a> followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III</a>’s death in 1702 and ended in 1747, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-prince-of-orange">William IV</a> became hereditary stadtholder of all the provinces. Towns governed themselves through closed oligarchies of regent families.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mare-liberum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mare-liberum.jpg" alt="Mare Liberum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mare-liberum">Mare Liberum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HugoGrotius-MareLiberum-1609.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hugo Grotius., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic showed that a federal, mercantile republic could defeat a great monarchy and act as a great power, and its institutions were studied across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-amsterdam">Bank of Amsterdam</a>, the transferable shares of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> and a public debt that Holland serviced at low rates of interest influenced English finance, especially after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> advisers and practices to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1688. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius">Hugo Grotius</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mare-liberum">Mare Liberum</a></em> (1609) and <em>De jure belli ac pacis</em> (1625) laid foundations of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. Relative freedom of the press made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> centres of European publishing, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes">René Descartes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baruch-spinoza">Baruch Spinoza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> lived and wrote, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek">Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</a> and the Leiden medical school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herman-boerhaave">Herman Boerhaave</a> advanced science. The painting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-golden-age">Dutch Golden Age</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt van Rijn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-vermeer">Johannes Vermeer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frans-hals">Frans Hals</a> and many others, developed landscape, still life and scenes of everyday life as independent genres. Overseas, the VOC’s posts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> and at the Cape and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">West India Company</a>’s colonies in New Netherland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> left lasting marks, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, founded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>, and the Afrikaans-speaking population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Dutch slave trading and the VOC’s violence in Asia, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> in 1621, are part of the same legacy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war.jpg" alt="Fourth Anglo-Dutch War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war">Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_the_Dogger_Bank_5_August_1781.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Luny, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From about 1720 the Republic fell behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Debts from the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> absorbed much of Holland’s revenue, the army and navy shrank, and the Republic stayed neutral in most mid-century conflicts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade did not collapse, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> remained a leading capital market, but rivals’ protectionism and the growth of British shipping eroded its share, while industries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>’s textiles declined sharply. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war">Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</a> (1780–1784), provoked by Dutch trade with the American rebels, devastated shipping and exposed the navy’s weakness. Defeat fed the Patriot movement, which demanded reform and attacked the powers of the stadtholder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-v-prince-of-orange">William V</a>. When the Patriots gained control of several towns and provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> invaded in 1787 to restore William V, whose wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmina-of-the-netherlands">Wilhelmina</a> was the Prussian king’s sister, and many Patriots fled to France. In the winter of 1794–1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> crossed the frozen rivers; William V fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in January 1795, and Dutch revolutionaries proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>, a French client state. The old federation of sovereign provinces gave way to the unitary constitution of 1798, and after Napoleonic rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> re-emerged in 1815 as a kingdom under William V’s son, William I.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Dutch Republic, a federation of seven provinces born of the revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, became the leading commercial and financial power of seventeenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, pioneered the joint-stock trading company and cheap public credit, and sheltered an exceptional flowering of painting, science and philosophy before British competition and French conquest ended it in 1795.</em></p>

